effect():

🧠 In Angular Signals, effect() lets you run some code automatically whenever THE SIGNALS THAT CODE
READS CHANGE.
Think of it as "Run this function now, and run it again whenever any signal I read inside changes."

    const count = signal(0);
    effect(() => {
        console.log('Count is:', count());
    });

when the effect() is created:

    Count is: 0

If we change the signal count that the effect code uses as: 
    
    count.set(1);

Then Angular automatically runs the effect again:

    Count is: 1